WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.930
About 230 million
hectares of black earths,

2
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:09.030
called Chernozems, lie mainly in the
steppes of Eurasia and North America.

3
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:14.790
Their black topsoil runs from about 20
centimetres to more than 2 metres deep.

4
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.610
Wheat, barley and maize grow here.

5
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.550
In the humid tropics lie
Ferralsols: red or yellow,

6
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.090
usually several metres
deep, under rainforest.

7
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.470
Deep soil, lush forest.

8
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.690
So why do some of the world's best
farm soils lie under grassland instead?

9
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.970
In this mission, you'll
read both soil profiles,

10
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.550
layer by layer, and link
each soil to its farming.

11
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.930
Then you'll diagnose four
threats to good soil:

12
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.930
erosion, waterlogging,
salt and compaction.

13
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:52.150
Your mission: explain why some of the
world's best farm soils lie under grassland,

14
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.460
not rainforest.
